  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "1b1a2717-3335-ec1e-422e-147bbc736cda",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "de86555c-46dc-8341-63c7-858f17b0b616",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Dr Nadeem was speaking at a women and wellness session hosted by the Working Group for Women (WGW) and Women in Medicine, a platform to support and promote Pakistani women physicians. The theme for the event was 'Time to focus on what matters' and brought in experts from areas of medicine and career management.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Experts talked about women's participation in medicine in Pakistan and the world over. Despite women making up over 70 per cent of medical college admissions in Pakistan, half of them never get into practice, said Dr Nadeem. She cited factors such as social importance of marriage and adherence to traditional gender roles as reasons inhibiting women to practice their professions.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Adding another dimension to the theme Dr Nargis Asad, an associate professor at the department of psychiatry, said that women have a triple burden to bear as they have domestic, societal as well as reproductive role to play. She stressed that to bring about a change in our societies, we will need change how we raise our young men and women.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Professor and Vice Provost Anjum Halai, Dean Medical College, Dr Adil Haider, and Muhammad Farhan, a human resource, business consultant were also present at the event. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
